      {
        "id": 40764,
        "name": "AvalonXQ",
        "username": "AvalonXQ",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/d2c977/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2012-10-24T14:22:15.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>One method of keeping track of cards that go in a particular order is to have sets of colored tokens numbered, say, 1 to 9.&nbsp; So you have a red 1 to 9, a green 1 to 9, etc.</p><p>Whenever a second card comes out that triggers during the same phase or conditions&nbsp;as an existing card, choose a color of token and put out the \"1\" and \"2\" tokens of that color.&nbsp; Then you can put out \"3,\" \"4,\" etc. as necessary.</p><p>For example, during the villain turn,&nbsp;Voss puts out is first minion that does end-of-turn damage.&nbsp; Later, during the environment turn, an environment card comes out that does something during the villain turn's start and end phases.&nbsp; You decide that red is a good color for&nbsp;the end of the&nbsp;Villain&nbsp;turn,&nbsp;so you take the red \"1\" token and put it on Voss's first minion, then take the red \"2\" token and put it on the environment card.&nbsp; If Voss drops another minion during his turn, it gets a red \"3,\" and so on.</p><p>If another card comes out that does something at the start of the villain turn, then you&nbsp;can&nbsp;choose \"green\" for that phase - put a green \"1\" on the environment card, then a green \"2\" on the new card.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T02:14:26.624Z",
